```apex
public class AccountRatingService {

    private static final Set<String> CLOSED_STAGES = new Set<String>{ 'Closed Won', 'Closed Lost' };

    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Map<Id, List<Opportunity>> openOppsByAccount = new Map<Id, List<Opportunity>>();

        for (Opportunity opp : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, Amount, StageName
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
            AND StageName NOT IN :CLOSED_STAGES
        ]) {
            if (!openOppsByAccount.containsKey(opp.AccountId)) {
                openOppsByAccount.put(opp.AccountId, new List<Opportunity>());
            }
            openOppsByAccount.get(opp.AccountId).add(opp);
        }

        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();

        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            List<Opportunity> opps = openOppsByAccount.get(accountId);
            String rating;

            if (opps == null || opps.isEmpty()) {
                rating = 'Cold';
            } else {
                Boolean hasLargeOpp = false;
                for (Opportunity opp : opps) {
                    if (opp.Amount != null && opp.Amount >= 100000) {
                        hasLargeOpp = true;
                        break;
                    }
                }
                rating = hasLargeOpp ? 'Hot' : 'Warm';
            }

            accountsToUpdate.add(new Account(Id = accountId, Rating = rating));
        }

        if (!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

### Key design decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **One `@future` call per trigger execution** (not per opp) | Stays within the 50-future/transaction limit regardless of batch size; eliminates redundant work and race conditions on the same Account. |
| **Deduplicate `AccountId`s in the trigger** | 200 opps spanning 10 accounts → one future call, not 200. |
| **Single SOQL in the `@future` method** | One governor-safe query for all affected accounts; no per-account query loops. |
| **`StageName NOT IN :CLOSED_STAGES`** | Uses a constant set so the closed-stage list is easy to maintain and the SOQL is a single indexed filter. |
| **`update` in bulk at the end** | One DML statement for all accounts; bulk-safe. |
| **Null-check on `AccountId` and `Amount`** | Prevents `NullPointerException` and avoids inserting nulls into the query set. |

## What I'd change and why

The instruction to call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` per line in a loop is a **SOQL-in-a-loop**. That method fires `[SELECT Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id = :productId]` on every call. With 200 lines per opportunity (and the realistic possibility of multiple opportunities in one DML), you'd blow the 100-SOQL-per-transaction governor limit almost immediately.

The fix: batch-collect the `Product2Id` values that actually need a discount, fetch them in **one** query, then call the pure in-memory method `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` for each line. This keeps all the business rules in `PricingRules` (single source of truth, no copy), requires no changes to that class, and is fully bulk-safe.

