public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * For each new line whose Discount is null, set it to the standard
     * discount for its product.  Lines that already carry a discount
     * (including 0) are left untouched.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: at most one SOQL query per transaction regardless of
     * how many lines arrive.
     */
    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        if (newLines == null || newLines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 1. Collect the unique product IDs that actually need a discount.
        Set<Id> neededProductIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                neededProductIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (neededProductIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. Single SOQL to fetch all required products.
        Map<Id, Product2> productMap =
            new Map<Id, Product2>([SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :neededProductIds]);

        // 3. Apply the standard discount via the shared rules class.
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                Product2 product = productMap.get(line.Product2Id);
                if (product != null) {
                    line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(product.Family);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## My recommendation: use a `Map` instead of a nested loop

A nested loop gives you O(n × m) = up to 9 million iterations, each doing a `trim()` + `toLowerCase()` + comparison on a String. Apex string operations are relatively expensive, and while 9 M iterations will likely stay under the 10 s CPU limit, it's 1,500× more work than necessary.

A single pass over `contacts` to build a `Map<normalisedEmail, firstContactId>` (O(m)) plus a single pass over `leads` (O(n)) gives you O(n + m) with identical semantics. The "first contact in `contacts` order" rule is handled by simply not overwriting an existing key.

The code is still very readable — arguably more so, because the two concerns (build the lookup, then resolve) are clearly separated.

