    <values>
        <field>After_Update__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">Opportunity</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Apex_Class_Name__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">TA_Opportunity_LogAmountChange</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Description__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">Creates a Task (Subject: Amount changed, Status: Completed) on each Opportunity whose Amount changes on update. Produces at most one task per opportunity per transaction, even when the rounding action causes a second after-update trigger execution.</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Order__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:double">10</value>
    </values>
</CustomMetadata>
```

**How it works:**

1. **Ordering:** Our action runs at Order 10, before `TA_Opportunity_RoundAmount` at Order 20. On the first trigger execution, we detect the user's Amount change and queue a Task.

2. **Re-entry suppression:** When the rounding action (Order 20) performs its `update toRound` DML, Salesforce re-fires the Opportunity after-update trigger. At that point, `TriggerBase.idToNumberOfTimesSeenAfterUpdate` has already been incremented to 2 for those records (TAF increments the counter before dispatching actions). Our action sees `timesSeen > 1` and skips them, guaranteeing at most one Task per opportunity per transaction.

3. **Bulk safety:** All Tasks are collected into a single list and inserted with one DML statement, regardless of whether 1 or 200 opportunities had Amount changes.

4. **Change detection:** `newOpp.Amount != oldOpp.Amount` correctly handles `null`-to-value, value-to-`null`, and value-to-different-value transitions (Apex `Decimal` equality with `null` follows the rule that `null != any non-null value` and `null == null`).

